=== layout: subsection

# 9. Aptamers and Ribozymes

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Binding and Catalysis

@body

- An *aptamer* is a short DNA or RNA sequence that folds into a shape able to bind a selected molecule.
- A *ribozyme* is an RNA with catalytic activity; a designed ribozyme can cut a target RNA sequence.
- Both require the right sequence, folded structure, target access, chemical stability, and delivery.

=== layout: two-panels

# 9. Binding Versus Cutting

@left

![aptamer and ribozyme figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/aptamer_ribozyme_legacy.png)

@right

[*Aptamer*]{underline=true}

Bind a protein, small molecule, or cell-surface target. It can block a target or act as a targeting ligand.

[*Ribozyme*]{underline=true}

Recognize an RNA sequence and catalyze a cut. The intended RNA and biological effect must both be measured.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![9. Nucleic Acids Can Recognize Targets](assets/set5_gene_therapy/aptamer_ribozyme_legacy.png)

@caption

