Topic 9: A Folded Aptamer Can Bind a Target

=== layout: four-panels

# 9. How Do We Find a Useful Sequence?

@top-left

## Start diverse

SELEX begins with many random nucleic-acid sequences.

@top-right

## Keep binders

Enrich sequences that bind the selected target.

@bottom-left

## Repeat and compare

Selection increases candidate binders; sequencing identifies them.

@bottom-right

## Test in context

High affinity in a tube is not proof of stability, entry, or action in a patient.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![SELEX cycle enriches aptamers that bind a selected target](assets/set5_gene_therapy/aptamer_selection_selex_2023.png)

@caption

Topic 9: Iterative Aptamer Selection

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Compare Them With an Antibody

@body

| Feature | Aptamer | Antibody |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Core chemistry | Nucleic acid | Protein |
| Recognition | Folded sequence makes a binding surface | Folded protein binding site |
| Design issue | Nuclease stability and delivery | Protein production and immune recognition |
| Possible role | Block a target or guide a cargo | Bind, block, recruit, or deliver |

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. What Would Convince You?

@body

- For an aptamer: show selective binding, then show that binding changes the intended cell behavior.
- For a ribozyme: show the predicted cut or target-RNA loss, then show the protein and phenotype consequence.
- Chemical modifications may improve blood stability, but they can change folding or function.
- The older diagrams show possible designs; each therapeutic claim still needs its own delivery and efficacy evidence.

=== layout: subsection

# 10. Cystic Fibrosis

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. Why Is CFTR a Useful Case Study?

@body

- Cystic fibrosis results from variants affecting *CFTR*, an ion channel important in epithelial tissues.
- In airways, altered ion transport contributes to dehydrated, difficult-to-clear mucus and chronic lung disease.
- CF is a powerful teaching example because the missing or altered function is clear, but reaching the right airway cells is difficult.

=== layout: two-panels

# 10. The Airway Is a Delivery Environment

@left

![cystic-fibrosis airway figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/cf_airway_legacy.jpg)

@right

- A treatment must move through mucus and reach relevant epithelial cells.
- It needs enough CFTR activity in enough cells for enough time.
- Mucus, inflammation, pre-existing immunity, and limited access to renewing basal cells all complicate delivery.

=== layout: two-panels

# 10. Three Strategy Families

@left

![CF gene-therapy figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/cf_gene_therapy_legacy.png)

@right

- *Gene addition:* provide a functional CFTR instruction.
- *Gene correction:* change a disease-causing sequence in target cells.
- *RNA replacement or modulation:* transiently supply or alter the message.
- Each must be judged by airway delivery, durability, and safety - not by mechanism alone.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![10. The Airway Delivery Problem](assets/set5_gene_therapy/cf_gene_therapy_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 10: The Airway Delivery Problem

=== layout: two-panels

# 10. What Did Early Viral Attempts Teach?

@left

![CF adenovirus figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/cf_adenovirus_legacy.png)

@right

- Early adenoviral studies made the delivery barriers visible: expression can be brief, and immunity can limit repeat dosing.
- The historical "30 days" text belongs to a particular older experiment, not a general current prediction.
- Today, ask for the vector, route, target cell, outcome, comparison group, and follow-up interval before interpreting a claim.

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. Choose Relevant Endpoints

@body

- Molecular: CFTR RNA or protein in target cells.
- Channel function: ion-transport assays or nasal potential difference.
- Physiological marker: sweat chloride where appropriate.
- Clinical outcome: a prespecified lung or patient-centered measure, compared with the best available care.
- A plausible gene-therapy mechanism is not the same as a demonstrated clinical outcome.

=== layout: subsection

# 11. RNA Payloads

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. Beyond Ordinary mRNA

@body

- Ordinary mRNA is translated and then degraded.
- Self-amplifying RNA (saRNA) also encodes replication machinery intended to make more RNA inside a cell.
- Circular RNA (circRNA) is joined into a loop; without free ends, it can resist some RNA-degradation pathways.

=== layout: four-panels

# 11. Design Tradeoffs

@top-left

## saRNA advantage

More protein expression from less starting RNA is the goal.

@top-right

## saRNA challenge

The replicase makes the RNA system larger and can create innate-immune-stimulating byproducts.

@bottom-left

## circRNA advantage

The loop can extend RNA persistence in cells.

@bottom-right

## circRNA challenge

Translation needs an engineered initiation strategy, such as an IRES-like element, and must remain reliable.

=== layout: two-panels

# 11. Which Measurement Fits the Claim?

@left

## Lower-dose claim

Compare protein output across matched doses and measure innate immune activation.

@right

## Longer-duration claim

Follow RNA and protein over time, then ask whether the duration matters for the intended therapeutic effect.

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. A Payload Is Not a Delivery System

@body

- Both saRNA and circRNA still need a carrier that reaches the intended cells.
- RNA integrity, contaminating double-stranded RNA, translation efficiency, and tissue exposure can each alter outcome.
- Decide whether the therapeutic goal needs a brief pulse, repeated pulses, or sustained expression before choosing a payload design.

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. Compare the Three Messages

@body

| Payload | Main design goal | Central limitation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| mRNA | Direct, transient protein production | Short persistence |
| saRNA | More expression from lower starting dose | Larger, more complex RNA and innate sensing |
| circRNA | Extended persistence | Engineered translation and reliable manufacturing |

=== layout: subsection

# 12. Gene Therapy Safety

=== layout: one-panel

# 12. Safety Begins Before the Dose

@body

- A product needs the right identity, purity, concentration, sterility, and biological activity.
- For a viral vector, that includes vector identity and limits on unwanted cellular DNA or protein carryover.
- For RNA, that includes sequence identity, integrity, formulation, and contaminants that could change immune sensing.

=== layout: four-panels

# 12. Four Questions for Every Product

@top-left

## Is it the right thing?

Confirm sequence, vector or particle identity, and dose.

@top-right

## Is it clean?

Measure sterility and relevant process-related impurities.

@bottom-left

## Does it work as intended?

A potency assay measures a biological effect tied to the proposed mechanism.

@bottom-right

## Where does it go?

Biodistribution measures target and unintended tissue exposure.

=== layout: two-panels

# 12. Potency Is Not a Generic Box

@left

## Match it to the therapy

- Enzyme therapy: restored enzyme activity.
- Channel therapy: restored ion transport.
- RNA-lowering therapy: reduced target RNA and protein.

@right

## Then test the consequence

- A mechanism assay is necessary but may not predict durable clinical function.
- Use controls that distinguish delivery effects from the intended payload effect.

=== layout: one-panel

# 12. Platform-Specific Monitoring

@body

- Viral vectors: immune reactions, persistence, unintended tissue exposure, and delayed effects.
- Integrating vectors: also follow insertion sites and long-term clonal behavior.
- RNA and particle products: delivery distribution, repeated-dose tolerance, and product integrity.
- Benefit-risk depends on disease severity, alternatives, dose, and the evidence for both benefit and harm.

=== layout: one-panel

# 12. The Last Study Question

@body

- What experiment would show that the payload reached the *right* tissue rather than merely some tissue?
- What result would distinguish temporary expression from a durable therapeutic effect?
- Which risk is created by the platform itself, even if the therapeutic gene sequence is correct?
@citation

FDA: https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/cellular-gene-therapy-products
